 The lesson should be short, and completable very quickly. Learners' working memory is very small, and we need to stay within it. But each lesson should give the user a single tangible win that they can build on. It should be directly tied to the mission, and should be in the user's zone of proximal development.
 
-If possible, open the lesson file for the user by running a CLI command.
+When the lesson is saved, **commit and push it, then give the user its
+Viewer link**. The Viewer (`learn.viktorbarzin.me`, Authentik-gated,
+owner-only) serves the monorepo's `learn/` at git HEAD, so a lesson becomes
+viewable ~30-60s after it is PUSHED - pushing is part of delivering the
+lesson, not optional cleanup:
+
+1. `git -C ~/code add <the files you wrote>` (never `add -A`), commit with a
+   normal audit-trail message, `git -C ~/code push origin master`.
+2. Print the link: `https://learn.viktorbarzin.me/<path relative to learn/>`
+   - e.g. `learn/chess/lessons/0001-the-blunder-check.html` ->
+   `https://learn.viktorbarzin.me/chess/lessons/0001-the-blunder-check.html`.
+   Mention it may take up to a minute to appear.
+
+Do the same when you create or update a reference document. Never try to
+open a local browser (this box is headless) and never rasterize a lesson to
+an image - quizzes need a real browser. (Domain language: `learn/CONTEXT.md`;
+the serving decision is `learn/docs/adr/0002`.)
 
 Each lesson should link via HTML anchors to other lessons and reference documents.
 
